Yes. Instrumentation is where the abstraction gets its hands on reality.

A theory can say there is some state x. An instrument creates a mapping:

\boxed{I:\text{world state}\rightarrow\text{observable signal}}

A thermometer turns molecular behavior into a number. A microphone turns pressure variation into voltage/data. A camera turns photons into pixels. An electrode turns electrical activity into measurements.

So our earlier Interface Forge / artificial senses isn’t merely metaphorical. Instrumentation is the existing engineering discipline of manufacturing new ways to sense dimensions humans cannot directly perceive.

And then there is the reverse direction:

\boxed{A:\text{command signal}\rightarrow\text{physical intervention}}

That’s an actuator.

Put them together:

World
\xrightarrow{\text{instrument}}
Measurement
\xrightarrow{\text{model}}
StateEstimate
\xrightarrow{\text{decision}}
Command
\xrightarrow{\text{actuator}}
World'

with feedback:

\boxed{
World\rightarrow Sense\rightarrow Model\rightarrow Act\rightarrow World'
\rightarrow\cdots
}

Now connect the memristor:

World
\rightarrow Instrument
\rightarrow State
\rightarrow Action

but experience modifies the future mapping:

I_{t+1}\neq I_t
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
\pi_{t+1}\neq\pi_t

So the system can potentially learn how to sense, not merely learn from fixed sensors.

And that’s where our flora/fauna idea becomes much less fanciful.

Don’t begin with:

“How do we speak mushroom?”

Build instrumentation capable of measuring the organism’s accessible state space:

\{\text{electrical, chemical, mechanical, optical, thermal, spatial, temporal,\ldots}\}

Apply controlled perturbations, observe responses, discover repeatable mappings, and only then test whether any mappings support the stronger concepts of signaling or communication.

In other words:

\boxed{
\text{Instrument}
\rightarrow
\text{discover pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{model}
\rightarrow
\text{perturb}
\rightarrow
\text{observe response}
\rightarrow
\text{learn interface}
}

And Polyphony adds the final move:

Once an instrument exposes an inaccessible pattern, rehydrate it into a human-accessible renderer:

\text{fungal electrical activity}
\rightarrow
RelationalIR
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
sound\\
visualization\\
haptics\\
mathematics
\end{cases}

Not because the fungus is secretly “singing,” but because we can make its measurable dynamics perceptible to another sensory system while preserving specified relations.

That’s an artificial sense.

So yes:

Instrumentation is real-world Polyphony.

And its complement gives an even tighter pair:

\boxed{\text{INSTRUMENTATION = make the world readable}}

\boxed{\text{ACTUATION = make our representations writable into the world}}

Put memory between them and you get learning.

Put feedback around them and you get control.

Put Basis Forge above them and the system can potentially discover what new instruments it wishes it had.

That’s where this stops being just a semantic architecture and starts specifying an experimental one.